{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">All Saints' is the parish church of Church Lench, the largest of the Lenches, and the church that gave the village its name. It stands at the top of the hill, with one of the best views in Worcestershire looking west towards the Malverns, and is one of the five churches of the ARCH Benefice. It is listed Grade II*.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>A short history</h2>
    <p>Church Lench was given to Evesham Abbey as early as 708, and a priest was already living here when Domesday Book was written in 1086. The oldest part of today's building is the 12th-century nave: a plain round-headed doorway of that time survives, now blocked, in its north wall. The south aisle was added in the 14th and 15th centuries, the clerestory windows high up in the nave are 15th century, and the west tower, with its battlements and corner pinnacles, was completed at the end of the Middle Ages.</p>
    <p>In the 1850s the Evesham architect Frederick Preedy restored the church, rebuilding the chancel and porch and adding the vestry and organ. Most of the windows hold his stained glass, put in between 1854 and 1867 and among the earliest glass he made.</p>
    <h2>What to look out for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The ring of six bells. The fifth is dated 1600 and carries the words "GEVEF THANKS TO GOD"; the others were cast by Taylor of Loughborough between 1869 and 1876, and all six were rehung for the Millennium.</li>
      <li>Preedy's stained glass, including the east window above the altar.</li>
      <li>The framed list of the rectors of Church Lench, which begins in 1269.</li>
      <li>The view from the churchyard towards the Malverns.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
